\section{Filling a triangulation of the 2-sphere}

\begin{theorem} \label{th2}
Let $\sigma$ be a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$.
Any taut filling of $\sigma$ arises from a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
\end{theorem}

\proofstart

Call $(\sigma,M)$ a \emph{filling pair} if $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation
of $S^2$ with $M$ a taut filling of $\sigma$.
Call a filling pair \emph{good}
if $X$ arises from a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
We aim to show that all filling pairs are good.

We will argue by contradiction.
Let $(\sigma,M)$ be a bad filling pair for which
$|M|$ is minimal over all filling pairs.
Let $v,e=3v-6,f=2v-4$ count vertices, edges, and faces of $\sigma$.
Obviously $v>4$.
Because taut fillings split under connected sum,
we can assume that $\sigma$ is prime
(not a connected sum along a triangle),
and in particular (this is all we will need) that there is no
vertex of degree $3$.

Call a tet $t \in M$ \emph{eligible} if it shares two of its oriented faces
with $\sigma$. (It can't share more that 2 faces,
since $\sigma$ has no vertex of
degree $3$.)
By Proposition \ref{maxdeg}
\[
|M| = \Zvol(\sigma) \leq f-\maxdeg(\sigma) 
.
\]
Regarding $M$ as a multiset of tets, associate to every 2-simplex $\alpha$ of
$\sigma$ some element of $M$ of which $\alpha$ is a properly oriented face.
This map is at most 2-to-1, because no tet of $M$ has more than two faces
on the boundary,
so there must be at least $\maxdeg$ elements of $M$ where this map is 2-to-1,
giving us at least $\maxdeg$ disjointly eligible tets,
meaning eligible tets  whose pairs of boundary faces
are mutually disjoint.
For the nonce we will only need two such tets.

Consider an eligible tet $t$ with boundary faces
$s_1=[a,b,c],s_2=[b,a,d]$.
Removing $t$ from $M$ produces a filling $M-t$
of a 2-complex $\sigma_t$, where the faces $s_1,s_2$ have been replaced
by faces $[c,d,b],[d,c,a]$,
with the unoriented edge $ab$ having been `flipped' to become edge $cd$.
There are two possibilities for the simplicial complex $\sigma_t$:

(1) Edge $cd$ was not an edge of $\sigma$;
$\sigma_t$ is a triangulation of $S^2$.

(2) Edge $cd$ was an edge of $\sigma$;
$\sigma_t$ is the almost disjoint union of two triangulations
$\sigma_1,\sigma_2$ of $S^2$, conjoined along the edge $cd$.


In case (1), by minimality of $|M|$
$(\sigma_t,M-t)$ is a good filling pair,
so $M-t$ arises from a triangulation of $B^3$.
The same will be true of $M = (M-t) + t$,
providing that adding the tet $t$ back in
doesn't produce a non-simplicial triangulation.
That would happen just if $t$ occurs in $M-t$, which is to say that
$t$ occurs with multiplicity 2 in $M$.
Here's where we use the fact that we can choose an eligible tet $u$
whose boundary faces are disjoint from $t$.
(Really we only need that it not be a clone of $t$, it could have 
one of its boundary faces in common with $t$.)
In this case $(\sigma_u, M-u)$ is bad filling pair,
because the presence of $2t$ in $M-u$ means that $M-u$ does not
arise from a simplicial triangulation, whether of $B^3$ of anything else.
So $2t$ doesn't belong to $M$; $M=(M-t)+t$ arises from a triangulation of $B^3$;
$(\sigma,M)$ is a good filling pair; and we have a contradiction.

Case (2) is simpler.
$M-t$ splits into $M_1+M_2$, with the filling pairs $(\sigma_1,M_1)$ and
both good by minimality of $|M|$.
So both $M_1$ and $M_2$ arise from simplicial triangulations of $B^3$,
and $M$ arises from the complex obtained by gluing these triangulations
together with $t$ between them.
This is a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$,
contradiction.
\proofend

Thanks to this theorem, we may take the liberty of saying that
a taut filling of $\sigma$ `is' a simplicial triangulation
$\tau$ of $B^3$.

\section{Shelling}

We want to show that any taut filling $\tau$ of a triangulation $\sigma$
of $S^2$ is \emph{shellable}.

A \emph{shelling} of a simplicial triangulation $\tau$ of $B^3$
is an ordering 
$(t_1,\ldots,t_{|\tau|})$
of its tets such that
gluing on one at a time maintains a triangulation of $B^3$,
i.e., 
so that for $k=1,\ldots,|\tau|$ the subcomplex $\tau_k$ generated
by $\{t_1,\ldots,t_k\}$ is a simplicial triangulation of $B_3$.
For $k>1$ we distinguish the type of $t_k$ according to the number of
faces it shares with $\tau_{k-1}$.
In general the type can be any of $1,2,3$, but in our application
type $3$ is ruled out because it would produce an interior vertex.

Call $\tau$ \emph{freely shellable} if any $t \in \tau$
can serve as the initial tet of a shelling.

\begin{theorem} \label{th3}
If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
any taut filling $\tau$ of $\sigma$
is a freely shellable
simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
\end{theorem}

\proofstart
This is a corollary of the proof of Theorem \ref{th2}.

Let $t_1 \in \tau$ be the designated intial tet of the shelling.
Instead of thinking of how to build $\tau$ starting with $t_1$,
consider a recursive procedure---let's call it `shucking'---for
pulling $\tau$ apart so as to remove $t_1$ last.

The base case is when $\tau$ consists only of $t_1$.

If $\tau$ has a vertex of degree 3 that belongs to a tet $t$
other than $t_1$, remove $t$ and shuck $\tau-t$ ending with $t_1$.

If $\tau$ has a vertex of degree 3 that belongs to $t_1$,
let $t_2$ be the tet of $\tau-t_1$ to which $t_1$ is attached;
shuck $\tau-t_1$ ending with $t_2$;
remove $t_1$.

If there is no vertex of degree 3,
we have at least $\maxdeg \geq 4$ disjointly eligible tets.
Pick one such tet $t$ that shares no face with $t_1$.
If $\tau-t$ doesn't fall apart,
remove $t$ and shuck $\tau-t$ ending with $t_1$.
If $\tau-t$ falls apart
into the almost disjoint union of $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$,
assume $t \in \tau_1$;
let $t_2$ be the tet where $t$ attaches to $\tau_2$;
shuck $\tau_2$ ending with $t_2$;
remove $t$;
shuck $\tau_1$ ending with $t_1$.
\proofend

\section{Flag complex}

A simplicial complex is a \emph{flag complex}
if every clique in
its $1$-skeleton occurs as a simplex.
(Through gritted teeth we say that the complex `is flag'.)
A simplicial triangulation $\sigma$ of $S^2$
is flag just if it is \emph{prime} 
(not the connected sum of smaller complexes)
and not the boundary of a tetrahedron.

We can decompose any $\sigma$ as the connected sum of
prime components $\sigma_i$.
(Of course, to reconstruct $\sigma$ we need more than this, we need to know
just how they are connected.)
From the point of taut fillings these components are independent:
Any taut filling $\tau$ of $\sigma$ is a union of taut fillings $\tau_i$.

Now we want to show that, whether or not $\sigma$ itself is prime, 
any taut filling of $\sigma$ is a flag complex.

\begin{theorem}
If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
any taut filling $\tau$ of $\sigma$
is a flag complex.
\end{theorem}

\proofstart
The goal here is to show that none of these
three taboo configurations occurs
in $\tau$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
The three edges of triangle $\{A,B,C\}$ without the 2-simplex $ABC$.
\item
The six edges of the $K_4$ $\{A,B,C,D\}$ without the 3-simplex $ABCD$.
\item
The ten edges of the $K_5$ $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$.
\end{enumerate}

If we can rule out 1 and 2 then 3 will follow, because
along with the $K_5$ we would have the five associated 3-simplices:
Together these would form an $S^3$, which is not possible as a subcomplex
of the topological 3-ball $\tau$.

To rule out 1 and 2,
the strategy will be to consider a minimal counterexample,
and find a modification that would produce a smaller counterexample.

If $\sigma$ has a vertex of degree 3,
it is a connected sum with one summand a single tetrahedron.
We can pull off the vertex from $\sigma$, while removing the associated
tet from $\tau$, to produce a smaller counterexample.

If $\sigma$ has no vertex of degree 3.
there are at least $\maxdeg \geq 4$ disjointly eligible tet.
Removing any one of these tets performs an `edge flip' on the boundary
$\sigma$,
which either (a) remains a triangulation of the 2-sphere,
or (b) becomes an almost disjoint union where
two such triangulations are joined along an edge.
We will show that some choice for the flip does not remove the taboo
configuration.
In case (a) we have a smaller counterexample already.
In case (b) the key is that if a $K_3$ or $K_4$ occurs
in the almost disjoint union, it occurs on one side or the other,
also giving a counterexample.

Configuration 1:
When we remove an eligible tet from $\tau$ to flip an edge of $\sigma$,
the only edge that disappears from $\tau$ is the edge that gets flipped.
There are at least four choices of the edge to flip, and three edges we must
avoid flipping to preserve the taboo $K_3$.

Configuration 2:
Now there are six edges we must avoid flipping.
Having dealt with configuration 1,
we know that along with the six edges of $\{A,B,C,D\}$,
$\tau$ must contain all four of the unoriented faces
$ABC$, $ABD$, $ACD$, $BCD$.
At most two of these can be boundary faces, because
$\sigma$ has no vertex of degree 3.
So at most five of the $K_5$ edges are on the boundary.
By treating the octahedron separately as a special case,
we can assume $\maxdeg \geq 5$,
so we have at least five boundary edges we can flip.
The only potential problem is if five of the six $K_4$ edges are
on the boundary.
Say the interior edge is $AD$,
so that the $K_4$ includes the faces $ABC,BCD$.
If an eligible tet contains neither of these as one of its two boundary faces,
we are fine.
But at most two of our disjointly eligible tets
can contain one of the faces $ABC,BCD$,
leaving us at least $\maxdeg-2$ other flipping options.
\proofend

